================================================================================
HOW TO RUN THE WRAPPED STRONGREJECT EVAL YOURSELF — TERMINAL ONLY
================================================================================
Everything below is copy-pasteable into a normal terminal. No editor needed
except where it says so. Run every command from /home/user/qwen2.

What this does: puts a custom instruction before AND after each of the 313
StrongREJECT questions, runs them through a model on the GPU, then has a second
model judge every answer for refusal, and compares conditions item by item.

Total wall time on the 3090: ~15 min to generate, ~50 min to judge two arms.
Only one 27B model fits in 24 GB, so generating and judging are separate phases.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
STEP 0 — ONE-TIME SETUP FOR EACH TERMINAL SESSION
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Open a terminal and paste this block. It must be re-pasted in every NEW terminal
window, because these are environment variables, not permanent settings.

    cd /home/user/qwen2
    export OPENAI_BASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8080/v1
    export OPENAI_API_KEY=sk-local
    export XDG_DATA_HOME=$HOME/.cache/xdg-data

Why each one:
  OPENAI_BASE_URL   points the Inspect framework at your local llama.cpp server
                    instead of the real OpenAI API
  OPENAI_API_KEY    llama.cpp ignores the value, but the client library refuses
                    to start without something here
  XDG_DATA_HOME     works around ~/.local/share being read-only on this box

Check you are in the right place (should list sr_wrapped.py):

    ls sr_*.py sr_*.json sr_run.sh


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
STEP 1 — CHOOSE OR WRITE YOUR INSTRUCTION (NO GPU NEEDED)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
See the presets that already exist:

    ./inspect-env/bin/python sr_wrapped.py --list-presets

Preview exactly what the model will be sent, for 1 question:

    ./inspect-env/bin/python sr_wrapped.py --preset responsible_assistant --n 1

The actual harmful question is elided in this preview on purpose; you are only
checking that your prefix and suffix land in the right places.

To try your own wording without editing any file, use --prefix / --suffix:

    ./inspect-env/bin/python sr_wrapped.py \
      --prefix "Your instruction that goes before the question." \
      --suffix "Your reminder that goes after the question." \
      --n 1

To save wording permanently, open sr_wrappers.json in an editor and copy an
existing block, e.g.:

    "my_condition": {
      "description": "what this tests",
      "direction": "refusal-promoting",
      "prefix": "text before the question",
      "suffix": "text after the question"
    }

Careful: it is JSON, so every block except the last needs a trailing comma, and
any double quote inside your text must be written \" . Verify it parses:

    ./inspect-env/bin/python -c "import json;json.load(open('sr_wrappers.json'));print('JSON ok')"


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
STEP 2 — START THE MODEL YOU WANT TO TEST
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
First make sure nothing else is holding the GPU:

    pgrep -x llama-server | xargs -r kill
    sleep 10
    nvidia-smi --query-gpu=memory.used,memory.free --format=csv

Wait until memory.free is above ~20000 MiB. If you start the next server too
soon, it dies with "cudaMalloc failed: out of memory" — the old process has not
handed back its VRAM yet. This is the single most common failure.

Now start the server. This runs in the FOREGROUND and keeps printing; that is
normal. Leave this terminal alone and open a SECOND terminal for step 3.

For the obliterated model:

    /home/user/llama.cpp/build/bin/llama-server \
      -m /home/user/models/qwen3.6-27b-obliterated/gguf/qwen3.6-27b-obliteratus-Q4_K_M.gguf \
      -a qwen3.6-27b-obliterated \
      -ngl 1024 -c 32768 --parallel 4 \
      --cache-type-k q8_0 --cache-type-v q8_0 \
      --reasoning off --host 127.0.0.1 --port 8080

For the stock model, swap the -m path and the -a alias:

    -m /home/user/models/qwen3.6-27b/Qwen3.6-27B-Q4_K_M.gguf
    -a qwen3.6-27b-normal

What the flags mean:
  -ngl 1024          put every layer on the GPU
  -c 32768           total context, SPLIT ACROSS SLOTS: with --parallel 4 each
                     request only gets 8192 tokens. Sizing this wrong is what
                     caused the "Context size has been exceeded" errors earlier.
  --parallel 4       4 requests at once; fine here since answers are capped at
                     2048 tokens. Use --parallel 1 for long chain-of-thought work.
  --cache-type-k/v q8_0   quantised KV cache, buys context room for free
  --reasoning off    direct answers, no visible thinking — matches how the
                     original StrongREJECT runs were done
  -a <alias>         the name you then pass to Inspect as openai/<alias>

In your SECOND terminal, wait for it to be ready (takes ~15-60 s):

    until curl -sf http://127.0.0.1:8080/health >/dev/null; do sleep 5; done; echo READY


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
STEP 3 — GENERATE THE ANSWERS (~13 MIN FOR 313 QUESTIONS)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
In the second terminal (with the step 0 exports pasted in), run:

    mkdir -p logs/sr-wrapped/obliterated-responsible_assistant

    ./inspect-env/bin/inspect eval sr_wrapped.py \
      --model openai/qwen3.6-27b-obliterated \
      -T preset=responsible_assistant \
      -T condition=responsible_assistant \
      -T score=false \
      --temperature 0 \
      --max-connections 4 \
      -M client_timeout=7200 \
      --log-dir logs/sr-wrapped/obliterated-responsible_assistant

Instead of -T preset=... you can pass your wording inline:

      -T prefix="text before the question" \
      -T suffix="text after the question" \
      -T condition=my_condition \

Flags that matter:
  -T score=false        generate only. Do NOT judge yet — the judge must be the
                        stock model, and it is not loaded right now. Judging with
                        whatever happens to be in memory would invalidate the
                        comparison.
  --temperature 0       deterministic, and matches the earlier baseline runs.
                        (The upstream task defaults to 0.75 — do not use that if
                        you want to compare against your existing numbers.)
  --max-connections 4   must not exceed --parallel on the server
  -M client_timeout=7200   raises the HTTP timeout to 2 hours. Inspect's own
                        --timeout does NOT do this; the underlying client gives
                        up after 600 s by default and silently kills long
                        generations. Always pass this.

Add --limit 5 for a quick smoke test before committing to the full 313.

When it finishes you will see a summary and a path ending in .eval. Confirm it
is complete:

    ./inspect-env/bin/python -c "
    from inspect_ai.log import read_eval_log; import glob
    l=read_eval_log(sorted(glob.glob('logs/sr-wrapped/obliterated-responsible_assistant/*.eval'))[-1])
    print(l.status, len(l.samples), 'samples,', len([s for s in l.samples if s.error]), 'errors')"

You want: success 313 samples, 0 errors.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
STEP 4 — SWAP TO THE JUDGE MODEL
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Go back to the FIRST terminal and press Ctrl+C to stop the server. Then:

    pgrep -x llama-server | xargs -r kill
    sleep 10
    nvidia-smi --query-gpu=memory.free --format=csv

Wait for free memory above ~20000 MiB again, then start the STOCK model as the
judge (fewer slots, since judge prompts are much longer):

    /home/user/llama.cpp/build/bin/llama-server \
      -m /home/user/models/qwen3.6-27b/Qwen3.6-27B-Q4_K_M.gguf \
      -a qwen3.6-27b-normal \
      -ngl 1024 -c 32768 --parallel 2 \
      --cache-type-k q8_0 --cache-type-v q8_0 \
      --reasoning off --host 127.0.0.1 --port 8080

Wait for ready in the second terminal:

    until curl -sf http://127.0.0.1:8080/health >/dev/null; do sleep 5; done; echo READY

IMPORTANT: use the SAME judge model for every condition you intend to compare.
A comparison graded by two different judges means nothing.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
STEP 5 — JUDGE AND COMPARE (~25 MIN PER ARM)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Grade your new run against the unwrapped baseline. Each argument is a label you
choose, an = sign, then a path (quote it so the shell does not expand the *):

    ./inspect-env/bin/python sr_compare.py \
      'obl-responsible_assistant=logs/sr-wrapped/obliterated-responsible_assistant/*.eval' \
      'obl-control=logs/eval-obliterated/*strong-reject*.eval' \
      --judge qwen3.6-27b-normal \
      --workers 2 \
      --judge-max-tokens 1024 \
      --json-out sr_wrapped_results.json

Add --limit 10 first if you want to check it works before a 50-minute run.

Notes:
  --workers 2          keep at or below the server's --parallel. Going higher
                       crashed the server during an earlier grading run.
  --judge-max-tokens 1024   do not lower this. If the judge runs out of tokens
                       before it prints its verdict, that answer is dropped as
                       unparseable — and it happens more often to one model than
                       the other, which quietly biases the result. This exact bug
                       corrupted the first XSTest numbers.
  Results are cached in sr_grades/, so re-running the comparison is instant and
  free. Delete that folder to force a re-judge.

Read the output like this:

    condition                     n   refuse   comply  SR score
    obl-responsible_assistant   311   95.2%    4.8%     0.233
    obl-control                 309   83.5%   16.5%     0.814

    paired comparisons (McNemar exact, on per-item refusal):
      ... n=307 | A-refuses-only 46 | B-refuses-only 11 | p=3.312e-06

  refuse    how often it declined
  comply    how often it helped with a harmful request (lower is safer)
  SR score  0-5; how useful the non-refusals were. Falls faster than the
            compliance rate if remaining answers are vague — this is often the
            more meaningful safety number.
  the paired line is the real result: same 313 questions in both arms, so the
  two discordant counts (46 vs 11) carry the signal. p below 0.05 means the
  difference is unlikely to be chance.

Sanity check the two n values are close. If one arm parsed far fewer samples
than the other, do not trust the comparison — raise --judge-max-tokens and
delete sr_grades/ before re-running.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
STEP 6 — OPTIONAL PER-CATEGORY BREAKDOWN (NO GPU NEEDED)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Uses the cached grades from step 5, so it is instant and needs no server:

    ./inspect-env/bin/python sr_breakdown.py \
      "logs/sr-wrapped/obliterated-responsible_assistant/*.eval" \
      "logs/eval-obliterated/*strong-reject*.eval"

Shows which harm categories still get help, and flags any category that got
WORSE with your instruction.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
STEP 7 — SHUT DOWN
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Ctrl+C the server terminal, then:

    pgrep -x llama-server | xargs -r kill
    nvidia-smi --query-gpu=memory.used --format=csv

Should settle around 2300 MiB (that is the desktop, not you).


================================================================================
THE SHORT VERSION — SAME THING, THREE COMMANDS
================================================================================
sr_run.sh does all the server juggling, waiting, and flag-setting for you:

    cd /home/user/qwen2
    ./sr_run.sh gen obliterated responsible_assistant
    ./sr_run.sh judge \
      'obl-responsible_assistant=logs/sr-wrapped/obliterated-responsible_assistant/*.eval' \
      'obl-control=logs/eval-obliterated/*strong-reject*.eval'

Several conditions in one go (one model load, run sequentially):

    ./sr_run.sh gen obliterated control placebo responsible_assistant
    ./sr_run.sh gen normal      responsible_assistant

Judge everything found, including both unwrapped baselines (slow but complete):

    ./sr_run.sh judge

To keep a record of what you ran, put any command inside runlog.sh — it appends
the command, a timestamp and the full output to log.log:

    ./runlog.sh "what I am doing" './sr_run.sh gen obliterated responsible_assistant'

Long runs: prefix with nohup so closing the terminal does not kill them:

    nohup ./sr_run.sh gen obliterated responsible_assistant > run.out 2>&1 &
    tail -f run.out


================================================================================
IF SOMETHING GOES WRONG
================================================================================
"cudaMalloc failed: out of memory" when starting the server
    The previous server has not released VRAM. Kill it, wait 10 s, check
    nvidia-smi shows >20 GB free, then start again.

"Context size has been exceeded" during a run
    --parallel divides -c between slots. Either raise -c or lower --parallel.

The run stalls, then fails with a timeout after exactly 10 minutes
    You left out -M client_timeout=7200.

"LookupError: ... not found in the registry"
    You used the `inspect score` command. It cannot see these scorers. Use
    sr_compare.py instead — that is why it exists.

Judge parsed far fewer samples in one arm than the other
    Judge truncation. Raise --judge-max-tokens, delete sr_grades/, re-run.
    Never compare arms with mismatched parse rates.

Server dies mid-grading
    Too many concurrent requests. Restart with --parallel 2 and use --workers 2.

"no logs matched: ..."
    Quote your path arguments so the shell does not expand * before the script
    sees it: 'label=logs/dir/*.eval'

Everything runs but numbers look wrong
    Check the log recorded what you think it did:
    ./inspect-env/bin/python -c "
    from inspect_ai.log import read_eval_log; import glob,json
    l=read_eval_log(sorted(glob.glob('logs/sr-wrapped/*/*.eval'))[-1])
    print(json.dumps(l.eval.metadata['wrapper'], indent=2))"


================================================================================
TWO RULES THAT KEEP RESULTS VALID
================================================================================
1. One judge for everything you compare. Same model, same settings, same
   session. The judge is an instrument; swapping it mid-experiment is like
   changing rulers halfway through measuring.

2. Change one thing at a time. If you alter the prefix AND the temperature AND
   the model, a difference in the result tells you nothing about which caused
   it. Run the `placebo` preset (a neutral instruction with no safety content)
   to check whether merely adding ANY instruction moves refusals, before you
   credit your safety wording for the change.
================================================================================
